      "seg_status": "edited",
      "source_text": "What we think Japanese government bond (JGB) yields have been rising alongside global yields, and the economy faces similar challenges with uncertain inflation and questions over fiscal sustainability. Japan’s inflation has looked relatively moderate in recent prints, but a part of it is due to energy subsidies. The extra budget needed to fund them is around USD 19 billion and funded entirely through deficit-financing bonds, which complicates the fiscal picture. On the currency front, USDJPY briefly fell into the 156-handle following an unprecedented coordinated U.S.-Japan intervention, but this intervention does not fix the fundamental issues driving yen weakness. The root causes of yen weakness are deeply negative real short-term interest rates and fiscal overhang. The government's costly energy subsidy program following the Iran conflict, together with ambitious fiscal measures such as increased defense spending and upcoming food tax cuts, are adding to concerns over Japan's already stretched public finances. While policymakers are prioritizing FX stability, these headwinds are likely to limit sustained USDJPY downside. We continue to see scope for a more hawkish Bank of Japan (BoJ) path than currently priced by markets. Stabilizing the yen may ultimately require another 3-4 rate hikes over the next 6-12 months, taking the policy towards 2%. Under this assumption, we remain neutral on USDJPY and expect the pair to trade broadly around the 160 level. More broadly, the yen is no longer functioning as a reliable safe haven. Japan’s heightened vulnerability to energy shocks, alongside a structural decoupling between USDJPY and interest rate differentials, has weakened its traditional defensive characteristics.",
